Prevent duplicate insertion when a POSIX HRT timer is rearmed during its callback.

hrt_call_invoke releases the timer lock while calling the callback. A work
queue awakened by that callback can call ScheduleOnInterval before the timer
thread reacquires the lock. That puts the entry back in callout_queue; the
timer thread then inserts the same intrusive-list node a second time. The
resulting cycle or lost queue entries can stop unrelated periodic updates.
GyroCalibration changes its interval on arm/disarm and exercises this path.

Before automatically requeueing a periodic entry, remove any insertion made
while the lock was released. sq_rem is safe for an entry absent from the
queue. Keep the requested deadline and period, including hrt_call_delay's
deadline override. One-shot rearming and cancellation retain their existing
behavior because they bypass automatic periodic requeueing.

This patch changes the POSIX timer implementation used by Linux SITL. It
does not change mission logic, timer rates, or the NuttX timer implementation.
Regression: python3 -m pytest -q verify/test_hrt_rearm.py

diff --git a/platforms/posix/src/px4/common/drv_hrt.cpp b/platforms/posix/src/px4/common/drv_hrt.cpp
--- a/platforms/posix/src/px4/common/drv_hrt.cpp
+++ b/platforms/posix/src/px4/common/drv_hrt.cpp
@@ -459,6 +459,10 @@ hrt_call_invoke()
 				//PX4_INFO("call deadline set to %lu now=%lu", call->deadline,  now);
 			}
 
+			// The callback may have rearmed this entry while the timer lock
+			// was released. Remove any existing insertion before requeuing
+			// it, so an intrusive-list node is never inserted twice.
+			sq_rem(&call->link, &callout_queue);
 			hrt_call_enter(call);
 		}
 	}
